The unified interaction model
description: one set of pan/zoom/select gestures across prose, images, maps, graphs, PDFs, and DjVu
- >
  Materials that can pan/zoom (images, maps, graphs) or that are long documents (PDF, DjVu, prose)
  all obey the **same gestures**, so muscle memory carries across them. **The primary thing owns
  the plain drag** - a selection on prose/images/maps, a node on a graph; panning and zooming move
  to a held modifier (**Ctrl** or **Alt** - either works, plus **Cmd** on a Mac), and the **right
  mouse button drags the canvas** too.
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - Gesture
    - 'Prose (text)'
    - 'Image / Map'
    - 'Graph (xyflow)'
    - 'PDF / DjVu'
  - ['**drag**', 'select text -> annotate', 'drag a rectangle -> annotate the region', 'move a **node** (rearrange the drawing)', '(PDF) select text -> annotate']
  - ['**Ctrl/Alt + drag**', '', '**pan** the canvas', '**pan** the canvas', '']
  - ['**right-button drag**', '', '**pan** the canvas', '**pan** the canvas', '']
  - ['**wheel**', 'scroll', '**pan vertically**', '**pan vertically**', 'scroll']
  - ['**Ctrl/Alt + wheel**', '', '**zoom** (around the cursor)', '**zoom** (around the cursor)', '**zoom** (page width)']
  - ['**double-click**', '', '', 'open the node''s **element**', '']
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - 'A **plain drag moves the primary thing** - on an image or map it selects (the cursor is a crosshair to signal it), on a graph it grabs a node. To move the canvas instead, **hold Ctrl/Alt and drag**, or **drag with the right button** (the browser menu stays suppressed inside the viewer).'
  - 'A **plain wheel** behaves like scrolling everywhere: it scrolls prose and paged documents, and pans an image/map/graph vertically (so a tall canvas scrolls past like text).'
  - '**Ctrl/Alt + wheel zooms** an image, map, graph, or PDF/DjVu. Canvases zoom around the pointer; PDF and DjVu scale the page width (then the pane scrolls).'
  - 'On a **graph**, a **double-click on a node** navigates to its element (a single click stays free for dragging it); the +/- zoom buttons sit at the top left, as on a map.'
  - 'Inline materials embedded in a **chapter**: an image or map chunk keeps plain-drag **pan** and lets a plain wheel scroll the chapter, so the surrounding page still scrolls normally (Ctrl/Alt + wheel still zooms the chunk). A **graph chunk is inert** - it neither pans nor zooms; **double-click its canvas to open the graph alone** (a double-clicked node still opens that node''s element). A marklower **embed** inside a prose chunk is a figure (alone on its line) or a chip that opens in place (mid-sentence); a video embed shows a poster and loads the player only once you click it.'
